
# These test benches are intented to validate the firmware written, especially when comparing
# to the implementation in simulation (which should match except the averaging 3/3/25 since the window
# is summed over 24 samples and then divided by 32)

# Instructions to run
command to analyze tb: 
be in the rtl directory

ghdl -a --std=08 defs.vhd
ghdl -a --std=08 upsampling.vhdl
ghdl -a --std=08 beamingforming.vhdl
ghdl -a --std=08 power_lut_8.vhdl
ghdl -a --std=08 power_integration.vhdl
ghdl -a --std=08 power_trigger.vhd

For a full trigger tb
ghdl -a --std=08 tb/trigger_tb.vhdl
ghdl -e --std=08 trigger_tb
ghdl -r --std=08 trigger_tb --stop-time=2048ns

For the internal trigger signals
ghdl -a --std=08 tb/wave_tb.vhdl
ghdl -e --std=08 wave_tb
ghdl -r --std=08 wave_tb --stop-time=2048ns

then move to the tb dir
and run V to plot signals
python3 plot_waves.py

# Some explanations if the io files and test bench files
input thresholds should be integers 0 <= thresh <= 4095 in the format
    beam0_thresh beam1_thresh beam2_thresh ... beam11_thresh

input waveforms needs to be formatted weirdly so the trigger module can decode the bit array
    to samples. See make_waveforms.py as an example. TO DO: The files may need to be front padded 
    with 128's for each sample and channel such that the thresholds can propagate from the 
    slow clock to the fast clock used in the trigger and no "dead time" on signal needs to be scrapped

    ch0_sample_set ch1_sample_set ch2_sample_set ch3_sample_set
    next sets ...

trigger_tb works by setting the control signals for normal operation
    and then reads in thresholds and test waveforms from text files
    each of the 'main' loop will read in a single line of the waveforms
    containing 4 samples per channel as one big int, with spaces seperating the channels
    then the output trigger signal will be recorded.

waves_tb will run the waveforms through the typical algorithm (upsmapling, beamforming, power integration)
    and will save the outputs of those to output_upsampled.txt output_beamformed.txt and output_power.txt

output_triggers contains a row for each clock cycle (or set of 4 samples) with some latency
    so if 1, triggers on 4 sample set some clock cycles align of
    if 0 doesn't trigger. clock latency tbd

output signals are recorded as they are in the modules. so one could measure propagation time through
each module. So when plotting the signals these will be delays from each other. If you want to see
the values as the are in sync with the input, you will need to figure out the offset... apply a clear
delta pulse

all signals can be plotted with plot_waves.py

